[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m assistant
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m [response] First, let's calculate the total number of periods John has in a day. Normally, a student has 6 periods, but John has 2 extra classes, making it a total of 8 periods per day.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m Each class is 40 minutes long, so the total time spent in classes each day is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m \[ 8 \text{ periods/day} \times 40 \text{ minutes/period} = 320 \text{ minutes/day} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m Since John goes to class for 5 days a week, the total time spent in classes during the week is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m \[ 320 \text{ minutes/day} \times 5 \text{ days/week} = 1600 \text{ minutes/week} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m Next, we need to calculate the extra learning time John spends on Saturday and Sunday. He spends 1/16 of his weekly minutes each on Saturday and Sunday. First, we find 1/16 of his weekly minutes:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m \[ \frac{1600 \text{ minutes/week}}{16} = 100 \text{ minutes/day} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m Since he spends this time on both Saturday and Sunday, the total extra learning time for the weekend is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m \[ 100 \text{ minutes/day} \times 2 \text{ days} = 200 \text{ minutes} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m Adding the time spent in classes during the week and the extra learning time on the weekend, we get the total time spent learning each week:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m \[ 1600 \text{ minutes/week} + 200 \text{ minutes} = 1800 \text{ minutes/week} \]
