[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m [response] To solve this problem, we will use Ohm's Law, which states that the voltage \(V\) across a resistor is equal to the product of the current \(I\) flowing through the resistor and the resistance \(R\) of the resistor. Mathematically, this is expressed as \(V = IR\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m Given that \(I = 40\) amperes when \(R = 3\) ohms, we can first find the voltage \(V\) using these values:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m \[V = I \times R = 40 \times 3 = 120 \text{ volts}\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m Now, we need to find the current \(I\) when the resistance \(R\) is \(20\) ohms, assuming the voltage \(V\) remains constant at \(120\) volts. We can rearrange Ohm's Law to solve for \(I\):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m \[I = \frac{V}{R}\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m 
