[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m At 8 p.m., Jane checks her farms and finds that they have the exact same population. If the population of Rod doubles every hour, but the population of Sphere is quadrupled every hour, how many hours ago did she start growing Sphere? Let's think step by step and output the final answer within \boxed{}.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m assistant
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m [response] Let's denote the time Jane started growing Sphere as \( t \) hours before 8 p.m. Therefore, she started growing Rod \( t + 5 \) hours before 8 p.m.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m The population of Rod at 8 p.m. can be expressed as:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m \[ 2 \times 2^{8 - (t + 5)} = 2 \times 2^{3 - t} = 2^{4 - t} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m The population of Sphere at 8 p.m. can be expressed as:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m \[ 8 \times 4^{8 - t} = 2^3 \times (2^2)^{8 - t} = 2^3 \times 2^{16 - 2t} = 2^{19 - 2t} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m Since the populations are equal at 8 p.m.:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m \[ 2^{4 - t} = 2^{19 - 2t} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m Equating the exponents:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m \[ 4 - t = 19 - 2t \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m \[ t = 15 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m So, Jane started growing Sphere \(\boxed{15}\) hours ago.
