[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m If \( n \) has exactly 10 different rectangular figures, then \( n \) must have 20 factors (since each factor pair \((a, b)\) where \(ab = n\) is counted once).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m The number of factors of \( n \) is given by \((e_1 + 1)(e_2 + 1) \cdots (e_k + 1)\) if \( n = p_1^{e_1} p_2^{e_2} \cdots p_k^{e_k} \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m We need to find the smallest \( n \) such that the product of these terms equals 20. The factorizations of 20 are:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m - \(20 = 20\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m - \(20 = 10 \times 2\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m - \(20 = 5 \times 4\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m - \(20 = 5 \times 2 \times 2\)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m The smallest \( n \) is obtained from \(20 = 5 \times 2 \times 2\), giving \( n = p_1^4 p_2^1 p_3^1 \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1466195)[0m Choosing the smallest primes \(2, 3, 5\):
